1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,456
Why do cats love boxes so much?

2
00:00:02,456 --> 00:00:06,204
You buy your cat a fancy new bed and it ignores it completely.

3
00:00:06,204 --> 00:00:09,960
Then it climbs straight into the cardboard box the bed came in.

4
00:00:09,960 --> 00:00:11,920
So what is it about a plain old box

5
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:14,189
that a cat just can't resist?

6
00:00:14,789 --> 00:00:16,641
We'll talk more about that in this video.

7
00:00:18,189 --> 00:00:21,277
A parcel turns up at your door on a quiet afternoon.

8
00:00:21,277 --> 00:00:24,105
You carry it inside and set it down on the floor.

9
00:00:24,105 --> 00:00:26,828
You open it up and lift out whatever you ordered.

10
00:00:26,828 --> 00:00:28,768
Maybe it's a lamp or a pair of shoes

11
00:00:28,768 --> 00:00:32,208
or a lovely new cat tree. And before you've even folded the flaps

12
00:00:32,208 --> 00:00:34,684
down there's a cat sitting inside the box.

13
00:00:34,684 --> 00:00:37,030
It doesn't matter how big the box is.

14
00:00:37,030 --> 00:00:39,689
It doesn't matter if the cat barely fits.

15
00:00:39,689 --> 00:00:41,869
A big chunky cat will happily cram itself

16
00:00:41,869 --> 00:00:45,434
into a tiny shoe box with its sides spilling over the edges.

17
00:00:45,434 --> 00:00:49,096
Then it just sits there looking extremely pleased with itself.

18
00:00:49,096 --> 00:00:51,016
Meanwhile the expensive thing that came

19
00:00:51,016 --> 00:00:53,560
in the box lies forgotten on the floor.

20
00:00:53,560 --> 00:00:57,160
People joke about this constantly. You can spend a small fortune on toys

21
00:00:57,160 --> 00:00:59,870
and the cat would still rather have the packaging.

22
00:00:59,870 --> 00:01:02,404
But there are real reasons hiding behind it.

23
00:01:02,404 --> 00:01:04,414
Some of them go back thousands of years

24
00:01:04,414 --> 00:01:07,334
and some are about how a cat feels on the inside.

25
00:01:07,334 --> 00:01:09,285
Let's start with the oldest one.

26
00:01:09,285 --> 00:01:11,245
Your house cat comes from a small wild cat

27
00:01:11,245 --> 00:01:13,767
that lived in deserts and dry grasslands.

28
00:01:13,767 --> 00:01:15,677
That wild cat was a hunter.

29
00:01:15,677 --> 00:01:18,841
But it was also small enough to end up as somebody else's lunch.

30
00:01:18,841 --> 00:01:21,261
Foxes and jackals and birds of prey

31
00:01:21,261 --> 00:01:24,152
and bigger cats would all have been glad to eat it.

32
00:01:24,152 --> 00:01:27,648
So a small wild cat needed two things at the same time.

33
00:01:27,648 --> 00:01:28,928
It needed a way to hunt

34
00:01:28,928 --> 00:01:31,487
and it needed a way to stay out of sight.

35
00:01:31,487 --> 00:01:34,842
The answer to both turned out to be a good hiding spot.

36
00:01:34,842 --> 00:01:36,592
Think of a gap between two rocks

37
00:01:36,592 --> 00:01:38,928
or a hollow under a thorny bush.

38
00:01:38,928 --> 00:01:42,774
Or maybe an old burrow dug into the ground by some other animal.

39
00:01:42,774 --> 00:01:45,991
Somewhere small and dark with walls on every side.

40
00:01:45,991 --> 00:01:49,661
Tucked in there the cat could rest without anything creeping up behind it.

41
00:01:49,661 --> 00:01:52,780
And it could watch the world outside without being seen.

42
00:01:52,780 --> 00:01:55,658
Cats are what scientists call ambush hunters.

43
00:01:55,658 --> 00:01:58,535
They don't chase prey for miles the way wolves do.

44
00:01:58,535 --> 00:02:01,552
Instead they wait patiently and quietly.

45
00:02:01,552 --> 00:02:04,500
They hide somewhere still and they barely move a whisker.

46
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:07,240
Then when a mouse wanders close enough they explode out

47
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:10,940
in one fast pounce. For an ambush hunter a good hiding

48
00:02:10,940 --> 00:02:13,669
spot is the most important tool there is.

49
00:02:13,669 --> 00:02:16,527
Now look at a cardboard box the way your cat does.

50
00:02:16,527 --> 00:02:18,702
It has walls on every side.

51
00:02:18,702 --> 00:02:21,158
It has one opening to peek out of.

52
00:02:21,158 --> 00:02:23,159
It's just big enough for one cat.

53
00:02:23,159 --> 00:02:26,104
Honestly it's pretty much the perfect hunting hide.

54
00:02:26,104 --> 00:02:29,909
Your cat sits in there with only its eyes and ears poking out.

55
00:02:29,909 --> 00:02:32,722
It feels safe and it feels ready for action.

56
00:02:32,722 --> 00:02:35,856
Anything that wanders past is about to get ambushed.

57
00:02:35,856 --> 00:02:38,726
And quite often that something turns out to be your ankle.

58
00:02:38,726 --> 00:02:41,003
The second big reason is stress.

59
00:02:41,003 --> 00:02:44,433
Cats are animals that like things to stay the same.

60
00:02:44,433 --> 00:02:45,613
They like the same food

61
00:02:45,613 --> 00:02:48,593
and the same routine and the same quiet corners.

62
00:02:48,593 --> 00:02:51,732
When something changes they can get anxious pretty quickly.

63
00:02:51,732 --> 00:02:53,272
A new house or a new pet

64
00:02:53,272 --> 00:02:55,796
or a loud party can all rattle a cat.

65
00:02:55,796 --> 00:03:00,238
And when a cat feels stressed its first instinct is usually to hide.

66
00:03:00,238 --> 00:03:03,081
Scientists have actually put this to the test.

67
00:03:03,081 --> 00:03:07,901
In 2014 a team at Utrecht University in the Netherlands studied cats

68
00:03:07,901 --> 00:03:10,462
that had just arrived at an animal shelter.

69
00:03:10,462 --> 00:03:13,384
A shelter is a really stressful place for a cat.

70
00:03:13,384 --> 00:03:16,519
Everything is new and noisy and smells of strangers.

71
00:03:16,519 --> 00:03:19,400
Some of the new arrivals were given a box to hide in.

72
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:21,132
The others didn't get one.

73
00:03:21,132 --> 00:03:23,990
The cats with boxes settled in much faster.

74
00:03:23,990 --> 00:03:25,430
Within a few days they were calmer

75
00:03:25,430 --> 00:03:28,161
and less stressed than the cats without boxes.

76
00:03:28,161 --> 00:03:29,631
They got used to their new surroundings

77
00:03:29,631 --> 00:03:31,766
and the people around them more quickly.

78
00:03:31,766 --> 00:03:35,163
Something as simple as a cardboard box made a real difference.

79
00:03:35,163 --> 00:03:37,968
So a box isn't just a toy for a cat.

80
00:03:37,968 --> 00:03:39,693
It's more like a safe room.

81
00:03:39,693 --> 00:03:42,413
When the world gets too loud the cat can duck inside

82
00:03:42,413 --> 00:03:44,089
and shut it all out.

83
00:03:44,089 --> 00:03:47,709
In there nothing can come at it from behind or from the side.

84
00:03:47,709 --> 00:03:50,484
It only has to keep an eye on one small opening.

85
00:03:50,484 --> 00:03:53,741
For a nervous animal that's a huge weight off its shoulders.

86
00:03:53,741 --> 00:03:57,116
That's also why cats love all sorts of other small spaces.

87
00:03:57,116 --> 00:04:00,543
Think about all the odd places you've found your cat over the years.

88
00:04:00,543 --> 00:04:03,927
Your cat has probably curled up inside an open drawer.

89
00:04:03,927 --> 00:04:07,867
It has probably squeezed under the bed or into the bathroom sink.

90
00:04:07,867 --> 00:04:11,507
Laundry baskets and paper shopping bags are big favorites too.

91
00:04:11,507 --> 00:04:15,358
Some cats wedge themselves onto a shelf between two rows of books.

92
00:04:15,358 --> 00:04:18,177
All of those spots share one thing in common.

93
00:04:18,177 --> 00:04:21,868
They wrap around the cat and make it feel snug and protected.

94
00:04:21,868 --> 00:04:24,473
And remember how much time a cat spends asleep.

95
00:04:24,473 --> 00:04:27,754
Most cats doze for a big part of every single day.

96
00:04:27,754 --> 00:04:29,614
An animal that sleeps that much is going

97
00:04:29,614 --> 00:04:32,098
to be very fussy about where it sleeps.

98
00:04:32,098 --> 00:04:35,138
A sleeping cat can't keep watch. So it wants a spot

99
00:04:35,138 --> 00:04:37,836
where nothing can sneak up on it while it naps.

100
00:04:37,836 --> 00:04:40,289
A box gives it exactly that.

101
00:04:40,289 --> 00:04:43,410
This love of tight spaces starts very early.

102
00:04:43,410 --> 00:04:44,410
When a mother cat is about

103
00:04:44,410 --> 00:04:47,022
to give birth she goes looking for a nest.

104
00:04:47,022 --> 00:04:50,751
She wants somewhere dark and quiet and tucked well out of the way.

105
00:04:50,751 --> 00:04:53,051
A closet floor or the back of a cupboard

106
00:04:53,051 --> 00:04:55,624
or yes an old cardboard box.

107
00:04:55,624 --> 00:04:57,634
If she feels the nest isn't safe enough

108
00:04:57,634 --> 00:05:00,964
she may even carry her kittens somewhere new. She picks them up one

109
00:05:00,964 --> 00:05:03,073
at a time by the scruff of the neck.

110
00:05:03,073 --> 00:05:04,893
So the very first home most kittens

111
00:05:04,893 --> 00:05:07,763
ever know is a small enclosed space.

112
00:05:07,763 --> 00:05:11,239
It's warm and it's dark and it's full of their mother's smell.

113
00:05:11,239 --> 00:05:14,370
No wonder a box still feels like home years later.

114
00:05:14,370 --> 00:05:17,058
And this explains the fancy bed problem too.

115
00:05:17,058 --> 00:05:19,298
A brand new cat bed is usually open

116
00:05:19,298 --> 00:05:21,693
on top and open on the sides.

117
00:05:21,693 --> 00:05:23,433
It sits out in the middle of the room

118
00:05:23,433 --> 00:05:25,433
where everyone can see it.

119
00:05:25,433 --> 00:05:29,266
It also smells of the factory and the shop instead of the cat.

120
00:05:29,266 --> 00:05:31,806
The box it came in might smell strange too.

121
00:05:31,806 --> 00:05:33,346
But the box has walls

122
00:05:33,346 --> 00:05:36,477
and a roof of sorts and only one way in.

123
00:05:36,477 --> 00:05:38,580
To a cat that's a far better deal.

124
00:05:38,580 --> 00:05:39,860
Give the new bed a few weeks

125
00:05:39,860 --> 00:05:42,220
and an old blanket that smells of home.

126
00:05:42,220 --> 00:05:44,819
Plenty of cats will warm up to it in the end.

127
00:05:44,819 --> 00:05:46,935
The third reason is warmth.

128
00:05:46,935 --> 00:05:49,337
Cats like to be a good deal warmer than we do.

129
00:05:49,337 --> 00:05:53,147
Scientists talk about something called the thermoneutral zone.

130
00:05:53,147 --> 00:05:55,427
That's the range of temperatures where an animal doesn't have

131
00:05:55,427 --> 00:05:58,436
to burn any energy to warm up or cool down.

132
00:05:58,436 --> 00:06:01,692
For people that zone sits somewhere around room temperature.

133
00:06:01,692 --> 00:06:04,052
For cats it sits a lot higher.

134
00:06:04,052 --> 00:06:07,061
It's roughly thirty to thirty six degrees Celsius.

135
00:06:07,061 --> 00:06:10,853
That works out to about eighty six to ninety seven degrees Fahrenheit.

136
00:06:10,853 --> 00:06:13,487
Most of our homes are quite a bit cooler than that.

137
00:06:13,487 --> 00:06:14,887
So a cat lounging

138
00:06:14,887 --> 00:06:18,323
in an ordinary living room is often a little bit chilly.

139
00:06:18,323 --> 00:06:22,028
That's why cats follow sunny patches across the floor all day long.

140
00:06:22,028 --> 00:06:24,028
It's why they sleep on top of warm laptops

141
00:06:24,028 --> 00:06:26,330
and pressed up against radiators.

142
00:06:26,330 --> 00:06:28,765
And it's one more reason they love boxes.

143
00:06:28,765 --> 00:06:31,918
Cardboard turns out to be a surprisingly good insulator.

144
00:06:31,918 --> 00:06:35,402
It traps the warmth of the cat's body inside the box.

145
00:06:35,402 --> 00:06:37,382
A small box holds that heat even better

146
00:06:37,382 --> 00:06:39,569
because there's less air to warm up.

147
00:06:39,569 --> 00:06:41,229
So when your cat squeezes into a box

148
00:06:41,229 --> 00:06:44,914
that looks way too small it might know exactly what it's doing.

149
00:06:44,914 --> 00:06:47,480
It has found itself a cozy little heater.

150
00:06:47,480 --> 00:06:50,803
The fourth reason is that boxes are new and interesting.

151
00:06:50,803 --> 00:06:53,881
An indoor cat lives in a pretty small world.

152
00:06:53,881 --> 00:06:57,564
It knows every corner of your home and every smell in it.

153
00:06:57,564 --> 00:07:01,161
So when a box arrives it's a genuinely big event.

154
00:07:01,161 --> 00:07:03,330
It has come from somewhere far away.

155
00:07:03,330 --> 00:07:07,286
It smells of trucks and warehouses and all the people who handled it.

156
00:07:07,286 --> 00:07:10,407
A curious cat simply has to go and investigate.

157
00:07:10,407 --> 00:07:14,654
And once it has investigated the cat often wants to claim the thing.

158
00:07:14,654 --> 00:07:18,288
Cats have scent glands on their cheeks and on their chins.

159
00:07:18,288 --> 00:07:19,688
When a cat rubs its face along

160
00:07:19,688 --> 00:07:22,547
the edge of a box it's leaving its smell behind.

161
00:07:22,547 --> 00:07:25,319
It's quietly stamping the box as its own property.

162
00:07:25,319 --> 00:07:28,914
Plenty of cats also love to scratch and chew the cardboard.

163
00:07:28,914 --> 00:07:32,623
Scratching leaves even more scent from glands in their paws.

164
00:07:32,623 --> 00:07:34,819
It also feels wonderful on their claws.

165
00:07:34,819 --> 00:07:36,919
So a cardboard box is a hiding spot

166
00:07:36,919 --> 00:07:40,354
and a scratching post and a chew toy rolled into one.

167
00:07:40,354 --> 00:07:42,863
Now here's the strangest part of the whole story.

168
00:07:42,863 --> 00:07:45,154
Cats don't even need a real box.

169
00:07:45,154 --> 00:07:46,474
You may have seen a trend online

170
00:07:46,474 --> 00:07:49,200
where people stick a square of tape on the floor.

171
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:52,393
It's just four lines of tape making an empty square.

172
00:07:52,393 --> 00:07:54,193
And very often the cat strolls over

173
00:07:54,193 --> 00:07:56,305
and sits right in the middle of it.

174
00:07:56,305 --> 00:07:57,665
It's as if the cat has decided

175
00:07:57,665 --> 00:07:59,898
that the square counts as a box.

176
00:07:59,898 --> 00:08:04,444
In 2021 a researcher named Gabriella Smith decided to test this properly.

177
00:08:04,444 --> 00:08:05,164
She ran a study

178
00:08:05,164 --> 00:08:07,725
with the help of cat owners in their own homes.

179
00:08:07,725 --> 00:08:09,385
The owners laid out shapes on the floor

180
00:08:09,385 --> 00:08:11,486
and noted what their cats did.

181
00:08:11,486 --> 00:08:14,322
One of those shapes was a real square made of tape.

182
00:08:14,322 --> 00:08:18,347
Another was a famous optical illusion called the Kanizsa square.

183
00:08:18,347 --> 00:08:22,102
The Kanizsa square doesn't actually have any sides at all.

184
00:08:22,102 --> 00:08:25,444
It's just four shapes that look like little round pac men.

185
00:08:25,444 --> 00:08:29,106
They sit in the four corners with their open mouths pointing inward.

186
00:08:29,106 --> 00:08:30,956
Your brain fills in the missing lines

187
00:08:30,956 --> 00:08:33,521
and you see a square that isn't really there.

188
00:08:33,521 --> 00:08:34,761
The cats in the study sat

189
00:08:34,761 --> 00:08:37,859
in that illusion square about as often as the real one.

190
00:08:37,859 --> 00:08:41,423
That hints that cats might fall for the same illusion that we do.

191
00:08:41,423 --> 00:08:44,883
Their brains seem to fill in the missing edges too. It also means

192
00:08:44,883 --> 00:08:48,095
that even the idea of a box can be enough for a cat.

193
00:08:48,095 --> 00:08:50,981
The internet already had a name for all of this.

194
00:08:50,981 --> 00:08:53,204
If it fits then I sits.

195
00:08:53,204 --> 00:08:57,124
Big cats love boxes too by the way. Sanctuaries that look after lions

196
00:08:57,124 --> 00:09:01,142
and tigers have filmed them playing with giant cardboard boxes.

197
00:09:01,142 --> 00:09:02,482
The huge cats pounce on them

198
00:09:02,482 --> 00:09:05,114
and squeeze into them and rip them to shreds.

199
00:09:05,114 --> 00:09:07,622
They do it exactly like a house cat would.

200
00:09:07,622 --> 00:09:11,358
That instinct runs right through the whole cat family.

201
00:09:11,358 --> 00:09:13,685
So should you give your cat boxes?

202
00:09:13,685 --> 00:09:16,311
You absolutely should if you can.

203
00:09:16,311 --> 00:09:18,311
A cardboard box is one of the cheapest

204
00:09:18,311 --> 00:09:20,742
and best presents you can give a cat.

205
00:09:20,742 --> 00:09:22,499
Just give it a quick check first.

206
00:09:22,499 --> 00:09:25,666
Pull out any staples and long strips of packing tape.

207
00:09:25,666 --> 00:09:29,666
Take out any plastic bags or loose ties. And cut off any handles

208
00:09:29,666 --> 00:09:31,750
that a cat could get its head stuck in.

209
00:09:31,750 --> 00:09:33,570
If your cat seems stressed it helps

210
00:09:33,570 --> 00:09:37,130
to offer more than one hiding spot around the house. A box

211
00:09:37,130 --> 00:09:40,641
in a quiet corner can do a nervous cat a world of good.

212
00:09:40,641 --> 00:09:44,402
That's especially true after a move or when you've got guests over.

213
00:09:44,402 --> 00:09:45,982
You can even cut a little doorway

214
00:09:45,982 --> 00:09:49,419
in the side of a box and put a soft blanket inside.

215
00:09:49,419 --> 00:09:53,547
Some people stack a pile of boxes into a whole cardboard castle.

216
00:09:53,547 --> 00:09:55,897
There's one thing worth watching for though.

217
00:09:55,897 --> 00:09:59,999
Hiding is perfectly normal but a big change in hiding is worth noticing.

218
00:09:59,999 --> 00:10:01,939
If your cat suddenly hides all the time

219
00:10:01,939 --> 00:10:04,280
and stops eating it may be unwell.

220
00:10:04,280 --> 00:10:07,278
The same goes if it stops using its litter box.

221
00:10:07,278 --> 00:10:10,122
Cats often hide away when they're sick or in pain.

222
00:10:10,122 --> 00:10:11,122
So if the hiding comes

223
00:10:11,122 --> 00:10:14,250
with other changes it's a good idea to call your vet.

224
00:10:14,250 --> 00:10:17,157
So why do cats love boxes so much?

225
00:10:17,157 --> 00:10:19,077
Because they come from small wild cats

226
00:10:19,077 --> 00:10:21,771
that needed safe hiding spots to survive.

227
00:10:21,771 --> 00:10:24,011
Because a box is the perfect place

228
00:10:24,011 --> 00:10:26,547
for an ambush hunter to lie in wait.

229
00:10:26,547 --> 00:10:30,558
Because walls on every side make an anxious cat feel safe.

230
00:10:30,558 --> 00:10:33,238
Because cardboard keeps a cat warm in a home

231
00:10:33,238 --> 00:10:35,313
that feels a bit chilly to them.

232
00:10:35,313 --> 00:10:37,973
Because a new box is full of strange smells

233
00:10:37,973 --> 00:10:40,475
and fresh cardboard to scratch.

234
00:10:40,475 --> 00:10:42,855
And because a cat's brain loves the idea of a box

235
00:10:42,855 --> 00:10:45,440
so much that a square of tape will do.

236
00:10:45,440 --> 00:10:47,780
So the next time a parcel arrives leave the box out

237
00:10:47,780 --> 00:10:48,840
for a while.

238
00:10:48,840 --> 00:10:52,219
Watch your cat climb in and turn around and settle down.

239
00:10:52,219 --> 00:10:53,939
It looks a bit silly to us.

240
00:10:53,939 --> 00:10:56,279
But to your cat that box is a hunting hide

241
00:10:56,279 --> 00:10:59,219
and a safe room and a warm bed all at once.

242
00:10:59,219 --> 00:11:02,729
And honestly that's a much better present than whatever came inside it.
